---

> AI Recipe-development note: This is my Thanksgiving adaptation of Wolf's Baked Custard recipe, not an official Wolf recipe. 
> Wolf's original uses 8 yolks, 3/4 cup sugar, 2 1/4 cups heavy cream, vanilla and salt, cooked in Convection Steam at 200°F for 30 minutes.

= Custard =

Position the modular rack in the middle position of the #Convection Steam Oven{} (CSO).
Preheat the oven to 200°F on Convection Steam mode.

In a medium bowl, #whisk the @egg yolks{8}(large), @pumpkin puree{1%cup}(100% pure pumpkin), @maple syrup{1/2%cup},
@brown sugar{1/4%cup}, @vanilla extract{1 1/2%tsp}, @ground cinnamon{1%tsp}, @ground ginger{1/2%tsp},
@nutmeg{1/4%tsp}(freshly grated), @ground cloves{pinch}, and @salt{1/2%tsp} until completely smooth.

Heat the @heavy cream{1 3/4%cups} in a saucepan over medium heat until it reaches a gentle simmer. Do not boil.

Temper the yolks. While whisking continuously, very slowly ladle the hot cream into the pumpkin-yolk mixture. 
This follows Wolf's custard technique of gradually incorporating simmered cream into the yolks while whisking.

For the smoothest possible result, pass the mixture through a #fine-mesh strainer{}.

Divide evenly among #(6) ramekins{}.

Set the ramekins directly on the #perforated oven pan{} and place them in the CSO.

Cook at 200°F Convection Steam, beginning to check at ~{30%minutes}. 
Because this adaptation adds pumpkin purée and therefore differs materially from Wolf's formula,
30 to 40 minutes is my test range, not a Wolf-published time.

Look for custard that is set around the perimeter while retaining a gentle movement in the center.
Wolf describes its CSO custard as set when finished.

Remove the ramekins, cool, then refrigerate until thoroughly chilled.

= Maple Whipped Cream (Optional) =

If desired, whip the garnish @heavy cream{3/4%cups} with @maple syrup{1%tbsp} and @vanilla{1/2%tsp}. 

= Serving =

Top each custard just before serving with a small dollop of the maple whipped cream, @toasted pecans{}(chopped), and @nutmeg{}(freshly grated).
